# 2026-07-30 progress notes — COMPLETE (both halves)

## First half: `all <store>` as a first-class row set
- A real Vec of the store's in-memory record structs, built by
  emit_store_all pushing each live (non-tombstoned) record through the
  normal vec machinery. Bindable, iterable, `.length`-able, passable to
  an unannotated (inferable-generic) function. The old emission returned
  a bare malloc'd array typed Ptr(Struct) with no length traveling with
  it, so `for u in all users` walked garbage and crashed. Conformance
  test covers empty/many/tombstone-excluded/pass-to-fn.

## Second half: queries as `Result of <row>, StoreError`
- Desugar in `lower_expr_store_query` (src/typer/expr/store.rs):
  `exists ? Ok(StoreQuery-row) ! Err(Missing)` — a Ternary over
  StoreExists, typed by `monomorphize_enum("Result", {T: Row(store),
  E: Enum(StoreError)})`. StoreError joins `current_fn_error_types`, so
  the pre-existing propagation machinery handles the rest:
  - non-fallible fn: bare bind is a compile error ("bare use
    propagates ... declare `! E` or handle with `? $ !! ...`").
  - fallible fn with StoreError in the union: `r is users where …`
    binds the UNWRAPPED row; a miss propagates as Err(Missing). No
    ceremony (DoD's implicit propagation).
  - `match <query>` and the quaternary (`? $.f ! fallback`) handle it
    explicitly. Write-through (`r.val is 999`), `.snapshot()`, and the
    @resource copy-rejection all still work on a match-bound row.
- Field read straight off the Result is a compile error
  (src/typer/expr/access.rs, scoped to enum names starting
  `Result__G_Row<` so generic-enum laxity elsewhere is untouched), with
  the two idiomatic forms in the message.
- The blocker that forced the 2026-07-29 revert was NOT a Row dual-
  representation problem: it was enum payload sizing. `type_size_of`
  had no `Type::Row` arm (fell to `_ => 8`), so
  `Result__G_Row<users>_StoreError` got an 8-byte payload for a
  112-byte row — ctor clobbered the stack, reads returned the name
  bytes ("Alic" as an i64 = 0x63696C41). Fixed by (a) a `Type::Row`
  arm in type_size_of resolving the `__store_N` struct, and (b) moving
  store declaration BEFORE enum declaration in mir_codegen/mod.rs so
  the struct exists when enum bodies are sized. The 8-19 pre-
  registration fix was necessary but not sufficient.
- Migration: ~70 sites. Script-migrated direct binds in
  bulk_tests/integration/store_index_persist/store_errors/
  store_compact/store_transactions (single-read → match, double-read →
  block arm); by hand: snapshot/write-through/coroutine bulk sites,
  audit fixture, 12 tests/programs/*.jn files, docs/jinn.md examples
  (+ new fallibility/propagation section; removed the two now-fixed
  "current limitations" bullets; corrected the stale 8-24 transaction
  limitation note). Conformance tests in tests/review_2026_07.rs
  (review_5_4_query_miss_*, review_5_4_unhandled_query_*).

## Recorded follow-ups (not in this task's DoD)
- `first <store> where …` (hir::StoreFirst) still returns a raw Row —
  it can also miss and should get the same Result treatment for
  consistency.
- Query BLOCKS (`r is users query / where …`) also still return a raw
  Row on the read path (tests/programs/query_parse.jn exercises this).
- Matching a raw Row against `Ok/Err` ctor patterns compiles silently
  and reads garbage as the tag (observed while migrating: an already-
  unwrapped row matched `Err`) — ill-typed match scrutinee should be
  rejected.
- Annotating a parameter `as Vec of <store-name>` resolves to the wrong
  struct — user-facing row type naming still needs a decision.
- The DoD says `Option of <Record>`; implementation uses
  `Result of <row>, StoreError` to compose with the store error union
  and `!! err` propagation — consistent with 8-22's insert/set Results.
